  "query_preview": "..\n\nA west-east axis of showers and embedded thunderstorms has begun to \ndevelop this afternoon along an broad axis of surface convergence as \ndeeper moisture lifts north. This area is currently along/near the M-\n59 corridor and expected to slowly lift north towards the I-69 \ncorridor through this evening. This mornings raob from DTX showed \nPWATs above the 90th percentile for this time of year and a narrow \npocket of elevated instability between 1-6km. Latest RAP analysis \nalso shows this area generally uncapped, thus this pocket of \ninstability is likely now surface based. While instability is not \nall too impressive, the warm, moist environment remains favorable \nfor higher rainfall rates with any thunderstorm activity. Rainfall \nthis afternoon and into tonight will be highly variable and \ndependent on where slow moving thunderstorm activity tracks. \n\nAfternoon surface and satellite analysis show both the surface and \nupper-level low across central Illinois. With the low already having\noccluded across the Midwest and the upper-level wave becoming \ncutoff, it will be very slow to move east over the next 24-36 hours.\nAnother wave of energy is progged to dig south across the region \ntonight and through Sunday, helping move the system along to the \neast. With these two waves of energy approaching the area through \nSunday, there remain uncertainties in how the potential for heavy \nrain and training convection evolve. There will be multiple rounds of\nlarge-scale ascent that move through the region, which will favor a \nfew windows of convection that look to remain loosely organized. \nThus, it remains very difficult to pinpoint where any axis of \ntraining convection could set up and bring possible urban flooding \nconcerns, so have opted to hold off on a Flood Watch. Expect at least\n0.5-1 inch of rain late tonight through Sunday. Locally higher \ntotals if we get into a training scenario with embedded\nthunderstorms.\n\nEarly next week, high pressure moves in behind the departing system \nand we should have a few drier days with seasonable temperatures in \nthe lower 80s. With warm air advection moving back into the region \nahead of another system tracking east across the upper great lakes \nand central Canada, we should see a gradual warming trend through \nmid to late week.",
